how. But I don't have T5 or T7. Unless I can get them. The unshown T5 has 4 left. So possible, but not certain.\n\nAnother possibility: B6 and B8. If I can get B7, then B6, B7, B8 would be a Chow. But B7 has 1 left. So maybe possible. But again, not certain.\n\nLooking at the valid actions provided: [Play T4; Play T6; Play W3; Play B1; Play T3; Play B6; Play T2; Play B2; Play B8; Play W2]. I need to choose which tile to play next.\n\nIf I play T4, that would allow me to keep T2, T3, T4 as a potential Chow if I can get T2 or T3. Wait, but I already have T2 and T3. If I play T4, I can't form that Chow. Alternatively, if I keep T4, maybe I can form T2-3-4 as a Chow. Let's see:\n\nIf I keep T4, then I have T2, T3, T4. That's a valid Chow. If I play T4, I lose that possibility. So maybe it's better to keep T4 to form that Chow. But I need to play a tile. The valid actions include Play T4. So if I play T4, then I have T2 and T3 left. But without T4, I can't form that Chow. So maybe not the best move.\n\nAlternatively, if I play T6, that leaves me with T2, T3, T4. If I can form a Chow with those. But I need to play a tile. If I play T6, then I can keep T2-3-4 as a potential Chow. So that might be better. Let's consider:\n\nIf I play T6, my hand becomes: W2, W3, T2, T3, T4, B1(2), B2, B6, B8. Then, I can form [T1-2-3] and [T2-3-4] as two Chows. Then, B1 pair. That would leave me with W2, W3, B2, B6, B8. Need two more melds. Hmm. Maybe W2-3 can be part of a Chow if I get a W1. But not sure. Alternatively, maybe B2, B6, B8 can form a Chow if I get B5 or B7. But again, not sure.\n\nAnother option is to play B1. If I play B1, I lose one B1, but keep one. Then, maybe I can form a Pung with the remaining B1 if I get another. But that's risky. Also, playing B1 would leave me with one B1, which can't form a pair. So probably not a good idea.\n\nWhat about playing W3? If I play W3, I lose that tile. Maybe not helpful. Similarly for other tiles.\n\nLooking at the game history, the ne